WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:05.782
This is the narrated briefing for a
companion guide in the Federal Modernization

3
00:00:05.782 --> 00:00:09.178
Workbook: Service Accounts,
from user accounts to group

4
00:00:09.178 --> 00:00:13.312
managed service accounts to
Microsoft Entra workload identities.

5
00:00:13.312 --> 00:00:15.874
The guide's idea fits in one line.

6
00:00:15.874 --> 00:00:18.819
A service account's password
is the one nobody changes,

7
00:00:18.819 --> 00:00:20.912
because changing it breaks something.

8
00:00:20.912 --> 00:00:25.042
Every step in the guide replaces a
password someone has to keep with

9
00:00:25.042 --> 00:00:28.876
a credential that Windows, or
Microsoft Entra I D, keeps instead.

10
00:00:28.876 --> 00:00:34.553
Every fact and command in this deck comes
from the guide, which footnotes each one:

11
00:00:34.553 --> 00:00:38.621
Microsoft Learn, read on November
ninth and eleventh, 2026,

12
00:00:38.621 --> 00:00:42.774
and for the managed service
account standard, NIST, FedRAMP,

13
00:00:42.774 --> 00:00:47.350
CISA, SailPoint and Microsoft
Learn, read on November twentieth.

14
00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:52.087
Rules that are the program's own, not
Microsoft's, are called design guidance.

15
00:00:52.087 --> 00:00:56.886
We'll go in four parts: why this matters,
what the kinds of service identity are,

16
00:00:56.886 --> 00:01:01.227
who decides and the standard every
managed service account follows,

17
00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:03.880
and how each step and migration runs.

18
00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:08.668
Here's the problem.

19
00:01:08.668 --> 00:01:11.886
The most common service
account in an older estate

20
00:01:11.886 --> 00:01:15.029
is an ordinary user account
that a service runs as.

21
00:01:15.029 --> 00:01:20.040
An administrator sets its password by
hand, under the domain's password policy,

22
00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.892
and it's rotated only if
someone remembers to.

23
00:01:22.892 --> 00:01:26.247
When that account holds
a service principal name,

24
00:01:26.247 --> 00:01:29.192
an S P N, it's highly
exposed to Kerberoasting.

25
00:01:29.192 --> 00:01:33.234
Microsoft Learn's overview of delegated
managed service accounts names

26
00:01:33.234 --> 00:01:37.143
Kerberoasting as the common issue
with traditional service accounts.

27
00:01:37.143 --> 00:01:42.780
Learn's Entra guidance on securing user-based
service accounts gives three ways to

28
00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.987
find them: an S P N on a user account,

29
00:01:44.987 --> 00:01:48.092
delegation, and a password
that never expires.

30
00:01:48.092 --> 00:01:53.036
Once you've found one, record every
host, service, application pool and

31
00:01:53.036 --> 00:01:57.806
scheduled task that uses it, every S
P N, and every group it belongs to.

32
00:01:57.806 --> 00:02:00.593
That record is what the
migration has to carry.

33
00:02:00.593 --> 00:02:02.340
So what could it become?

34
00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:08.917
The guide compares twelve kinds of service

35
00:02:08.917 --> 00:02:11.665
identity in one table;
here's the short form.

36
00:02:11.665 --> 00:02:16.080
On premises, Microsoft Learn names
the user account used as a service,

37
00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:20.960
the standalone managed service account,
the group managed service account,

38
00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:25.452
and, new in Windows Server 2025, the
delegated managed service account.

39
00:02:25.452 --> 00:02:30.705
A standalone or group account has a
two-hundred-forty-byte random password that

40
00:02:30.705 --> 00:02:35.957
Windows changes every thirty days by default;
for a G M S A, the domain controllers

41
00:02:35.957 --> 00:02:40.891
compute it from the K D S root key, and
the hosts allowed to retrieve it hold it.

42
00:02:40.891 --> 00:02:43.542
A D M S A ties its secret to the machine,

43
00:02:43.542 --> 00:02:46.990
and Learn says it helps
prevent Kerberoasting.

44
00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:50.722
Virtual and computer accounts act
as the computer on the network,

45
00:02:50.722 --> 00:02:53.003
so you can't tell which service used them.

46
00:02:53.003 --> 00:02:58.128
In Microsoft Entra I D: an app registration
with a certificate you keep in a vault;

47
00:02:58.128 --> 00:03:03.099
system- and user-assigned managed
identities, whose credentials Azure keeps;

48
00:03:03.099 --> 00:03:07.914
an Azure Arc-enabled server's own
managed identity; a federated credential,

49
00:03:07.914 --> 00:03:11.098
signed by an outside issuer;
and agent identities,

50
00:03:11.098 --> 00:03:14.127
which take their credentials
from a blueprint.

51
00:03:14.127 --> 00:03:17.090
None of the cloud kinds speaks Kerberos.

52
00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:22.665
Three things follow from that table.

53
00:03:22.665 --> 00:03:27.315
First, nothing in Microsoft Entra
I D speaks Kerberos for a service.

54
00:03:27.315 --> 00:03:32.621
A workload that authenticates with Kerberos
or N T L M to an on-premises resource,

55
00:03:32.621 --> 00:03:37.927
a file share, a SQL Server login mapped
to a Windows account, or a Kerberos-only

56
00:03:37.927 --> 00:03:43.153
application, keeps an Active Directory
identity until that resource changes.

57
00:03:43.153 --> 00:03:48.015
Second, a G M S A is a better
password, not a different protocol.

58
00:03:48.015 --> 00:03:52.545
It's long, random and rotated, but
it still exists, every host allowed

59
00:03:52.545 --> 00:03:57.240
to retrieve it holds it, and it works
only while Active Directory does.

60
00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:00.247
Third, managed identities
are the end state

61
00:04:00.247 --> 00:04:03.003
only on Azure or on an Arc-enabled server.

62
00:04:03.003 --> 00:04:06.450
So a hybrid workload keeps
its G M S A for Kerberos

63
00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:09.640
and gets a cloud identity
for its cloud calls.

64
00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:11.915
Learn also documents special cases.

65
00:04:11.915 --> 00:04:14.791
Failover clusters don't support G M S As,

66
00:04:14.791 --> 00:04:18.565
though services on the
Cluster service can use one.

67
00:04:18.565 --> 00:04:23.090
In Entra Domain Services, the
K D S root key is pre-created.

68
00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:25.508
Cloud Sync's agent runs as a G M S A,

69
00:04:25.508 --> 00:04:30.620
and Connect Sync's account can
be chosen only at installation.

70
00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:35.707
Now, who decides.

71
00:04:35.707 --> 00:04:38.759
The guide's decision
path is nine questions,

72
00:04:38.759 --> 00:04:41.895
asked in order, and the
first yes is the target.

73
00:04:41.895 --> 00:04:45.447
Each target lines up with a
disposition the application

74
00:04:45.447 --> 00:04:48.395
owner records in the
Non-Person Identity track.

75
00:04:48.395 --> 00:04:52.645
One: is the account disabled,
unused or without an owner?

76
00:04:52.645 --> 00:04:53.645
Retire it.

77
00:04:53.645 --> 00:04:58.307
Two: does the workload run on an Azure
resource, or is it moving to one?

78
00:04:58.307 --> 00:05:01.246
A managed identity,
user-assigned preferred.

79
00:05:01.246 --> 00:05:07.133
Three: does it run in C I C D or on a
platform with its own O I D C issuer?

80
00:05:07.133 --> 00:05:08.657
A federated credential.

81
00:05:08.657 --> 00:05:13.532
Four: does it stay on premises
but call Graph or Azure A P I's?

82
00:05:13.532 --> 00:05:18.357
The Arc-enabled server's identity, or
an app registration with a certificate.

83
00:05:18.357 --> 00:05:24.147
Five: does it need Kerberos, with a
Windows Server 2025 domain controller,

84
00:05:24.147 --> 00:05:28.296
every host on 2025, and no
unconstrained delegation?

85
00:05:28.296 --> 00:05:28.942
A D M S A.

86
00:05:28.942 --> 00:05:31.142
Six: otherwise, a G M S A.

87
00:05:31.142 --> 00:05:37.870
Seven and eight keep what must
stay, with an owner and an end date.

88
00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:39.745
Nine is the Kerberos island.

89
00:05:39.745 --> 00:05:44.209
This lines up with Microsoft Learn:
use a G M S A unless the service

90
00:05:44.209 --> 00:05:49.120
doesn't support one, then a standalone
account, then a user account.

91
00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:55.700
These are the program's
rules, design guidance,

92
00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:58.008
and they apply to a hand migration too.

93
00:05:58.008 --> 00:06:02.977
Every identity has two named technical
owners and a sponsor who is a federal

94
00:06:02.977 --> 00:06:06.053
employee mastered in H R;
a contractor can build

95
00:06:06.053 --> 00:06:08.971
or run a workload but
can't be its only sponsor.

96
00:06:08.971 --> 00:06:12.108
No account moves without
its reviewed disposition.

97
00:06:12.108 --> 00:06:16.801
The old account runs in parallel, and
it's disabled no sooner than thirty days

98
00:06:16.801 --> 00:06:21.420
after the switch; it's deleted only
later, through the decommission process.

99
00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:25.117
No client secrets: a cloud
identity is a managed identity,

100
00:06:25.117 --> 00:06:28.656
a federated credential or a
certificate, in that order,

101
00:06:28.656 --> 00:06:32.432
and Microsoft Learn says not
to use password credentials.

102
00:06:32.432 --> 00:06:37.108
No privileged groups: Learn says service
accounts shouldn't be members of them,

103
00:06:37.108 --> 00:06:39.770
and each service should
have its own account.

104
00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:45.132
And changes are reviewed: in production,
each write is a plan approved by its hash.

105
00:06:45.132 --> 00:06:47.507
One place where Learn's own pages differ.

106
00:06:47.507 --> 00:06:52.171
Learn's Entra page on group managed service
accounts ends its move by deleting the

107
00:06:52.171 --> 00:06:55.051
old account, while its page
on setting up delegated

108
00:06:55.051 --> 00:06:57.520
accounts says never to
delete the original.

109
00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:00.250
The program follows the stricter reading.

110
00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:08.723
The guide now carries a standard that
every managed service account follows,

111
00:07:08.723 --> 00:07:12.412
from request to retirement, with
the same evidence each time.

112
00:07:12.412 --> 00:07:15.480
It's the program's
process, design guidance,

113
00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:20.213
and its rule fits in one line:
machines run the steps; people decide.

114
00:07:20.213 --> 00:07:24.569
Every step a script can run, it
runs: the request's checks, the plan,

115
00:07:24.569 --> 00:07:28.926
the Active Directory writes, the
re-read that closes the ticket,

116
00:07:28.926 --> 00:07:32.612
the monitoring, the
certifications and the evidence.

117
00:07:32.612 --> 00:07:35.735
People decide, approve in
SailPoint, accept risk,

118
00:07:35.735 --> 00:07:40.263
and run the few steps that need Domain
Admins or that delete something.

119
00:07:40.263 --> 00:07:40.950
Why?

120
00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:45.966
NIST special publication eight hundred
fifty-three has enhancements written for it,

121
00:07:45.966 --> 00:07:47.803
automated account management,

122
00:07:47.803 --> 00:07:51.900
disabling accounts and automated
audit, all in the Moderate baseline.

123
00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:56.762
FedRAMP's indicators name automating
account management and secret management.

124
00:07:56.762 --> 00:08:00.131
CISA's Zero Trust Maturity
Model makes automation

125
00:08:00.131 --> 00:08:03.187
and orchestration a
cross-cutting capability.

126
00:08:03.187 --> 00:08:07.599
And a script leaves a record a person
can't: the approved plan's hash,

127
00:08:07.599 --> 00:08:11.112
the exact call, the re-read
and a hashed evidence file.

128
00:08:11.112 --> 00:08:15.577
The standard keeps automation's limits:
approvals still come from people,

129
00:08:15.577 --> 00:08:20.189
separation of duties keeps the approver,
the pipeline's owner and the person

130
00:08:20.189 --> 00:08:24.800
who deletes apart, and the pipeline's
own identity gets the narrowest rights.

131
00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:28.780
Automation supports a requirement;
the assessor decides.

132
00:08:32.530 --> 00:08:33.480
Who does what.

133
00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:38.453
An owner asks for, changes or retires an
account in the agency's system access

134
00:08:38.453 --> 00:08:42.005
management tool, which hands
the request to SailPoint

135
00:08:42.005 --> 00:08:45.005
and never approves or
writes anything itself.

136
00:08:45.005 --> 00:08:48.760
SailPoint approves every
request, whatever its risk tier,

137
00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:52.672
aggregates the accounts from
Active Directory and Entra I D,

138
00:08:52.672 --> 00:08:56.193
correlates their owners,
and runs the certifications.

139
00:08:56.193 --> 00:08:57.849
It never writes a G M S A.

140
00:08:57.849 --> 00:09:02.361
ServiceNow holds the change
record, the typed findings and

141
00:09:02.361 --> 00:09:06.780
the decommission ticket, and
closes a task only on a re-read.

142
00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:10.722
The pipeline runs the approved
plan: it creates the G M S A,

143
00:09:10.722 --> 00:09:15.692
sets its retriever group, and disables
and moves a superseded account.

144
00:09:15.692 --> 00:09:20.342
It never runs a D M S A step,
an uninstall or a delete.

145
00:09:20.342 --> 00:09:24.460
Those are a person's: a Domain
Admin runs the D M S A steps,

146
00:09:24.460 --> 00:09:27.267
and a person uninstalls and deletes.

147
00:09:27.267 --> 00:09:30.830
No person clicks through a portal
to do what the pipeline does.

148
00:09:30.830 --> 00:09:35.747
The pipeline's own identity, G M S A
dash M S A pipe, is itself a G M S A,

149
00:09:35.747 --> 00:09:41.147
with only the rights its calls need:
membership of the creator group,

150
00:09:41.147 --> 00:09:47.318
D L G M S A Creators, write of the retriever
attribute on the accounts it creates,

151
00:09:47.318 --> 00:09:52.042
and disable and move into the
holding organizational unit.

152
00:09:52.042 --> 00:09:56.040
It has two owners, a sponsor
and its own certification.

153
00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:00.765
The settings.

154
00:10:00.765 --> 00:10:05.044
A G M S A is named G M S A dash
and the system's short name,

155
00:10:05.044 --> 00:10:09.527
fifteen characters or fewer
and unique in the forest.

156
00:10:09.527 --> 00:10:13.215
It lives in a dedicated
service-account organizational unit.

157
00:10:13.215 --> 00:10:18.524
Learn's Entra page says G M S As belong in
the Managed Service Accounts container,

158
00:10:18.524 --> 00:10:23.351
but the cmdlet takes any O U, and the
program's own O U keeps the create right,

159
00:10:23.351 --> 00:10:27.453
Group Policy and SailPoint's
scope on service accounts alone.

160
00:10:27.453 --> 00:10:32.252
Only the creator group may create one,
and the pipeline is its only member.

161
00:10:32.252 --> 00:10:36.490
One domain local group of the workload's
hosts retrieves its password.

162
00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:39.602
A E S two-fifty-six only; thirty days,

163
00:10:39.602 --> 00:10:43.715
stated at creation;
outbound-only with no S P N.

164
00:10:43.715 --> 00:10:47.661
Two owners and a federal-employee
sponsor, no privileged group,

165
00:10:47.661 --> 00:10:51.228
and a SailPoint certification
on the risk tier's cadence.

166
00:10:51.228 --> 00:10:56.107
A disabled account waits ninety days, with
no sign-in seen, before it's deleted;

167
00:10:56.107 --> 00:11:00.677
Defender for Identity's stale-account
assessment uses ninety days too.

168
00:11:00.677 --> 00:11:05.478
Setup happens once: the K D S root
key, the two O Us, then a Domain Admin

169
00:11:05.478 --> 00:11:10.634
creates the creator group and delegates
the create right with D S A C L S.

170
00:11:10.634 --> 00:11:13.211
Learn gives no delegation command,

171
00:11:13.211 --> 00:11:16.678
so run it in the test domain
first and confirm it.

172
00:11:16.678 --> 00:11:20.101
Then the pipeline's own G
M S A, SailPoint's Active

173
00:11:20.101 --> 00:11:24.860
Directory source scoped to the
O U, and Defender for Identity.

174
00:11:28.610 --> 00:11:31.788
Every step carries its
federal requirement,

175
00:11:31.788 --> 00:11:35.910
the indicator it aligns to, and
the evidence the I S S O keeps.

176
00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:39.570
The inventory supports A
C two, account management,

177
00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:42.272
and C M eight, the component inventory.

178
00:11:42.272 --> 00:11:46.787
The K D S root key supports I A
five, authenticator management,

179
00:11:46.787 --> 00:11:51.211
with event forty-oh-four and the
key's start time as evidence.

180
00:11:51.211 --> 00:11:56.683
Creating the account supports A C six,
least privilege, I A five, and C M three,

181
00:11:56.683 --> 00:12:02.785
configuration change control: the evidence
is the approved plan and the read-back.

182
00:12:02.785 --> 00:12:07.724
Installing and switching supports least
privilege and privileged accounts:

183
00:12:07.724 --> 00:12:10.991
Test-A D Service Account on
each host, the S P N listing,

184
00:12:10.991 --> 00:12:13.498
and the service's managed-account check.

185
00:12:13.498 --> 00:12:16.386
The parallel run supports
disabling accounts.

186
00:12:16.386 --> 00:12:21.979
The cloud identities support I A nine,
service identification and authentication,

187
00:12:21.979 --> 00:12:24.897
which sits in no baseline
and is tailored in.

188
00:12:24.897 --> 00:12:28.394
The controls are from the NIST
special publication eight

189
00:12:28.394 --> 00:12:31.672
hundred fifty-three, revision
five, Moderate baseline.

190
00:12:31.672 --> 00:12:35.830
K S I's are fed-ramp twenty X
indicators for cloud service providers;

191
00:12:35.830 --> 00:12:41.410
which evidence supports which indicator is
the program's mapping, design guidance.

192
00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:48.355
Now the standard step by
step, each with who runs it,

193
00:12:48.355 --> 00:12:51.160
the control it supports
and its indicators.

194
00:12:51.160 --> 00:12:54.801
S1: the owner asks, and the
developer workbook's Chapter

195
00:12:54.801 --> 00:12:58.685
12 checks every field before
SailPoint's approvers see it.

196
00:12:58.685 --> 00:13:01.823
S2: the decision path gives the target.

197
00:13:01.823 --> 00:13:06.710
S3: SailPoint approves, and
ServiceNow opens the change record.

198
00:13:06.710 --> 00:13:12.104
S4: the pipeline runs the approved plan
and creates the G M S A to the standard;

199
00:13:12.104 --> 00:13:15.410
that supports automated
account management.

200
00:13:15.410 --> 00:13:21.447
S5: each host installs and tests it, and
Group Policy limits log on as a service.

201
00:13:21.447 --> 00:13:26.609
S6: SailPoint aggregates it, and
the ticket closes only on a re-read.

202
00:13:26.609 --> 00:13:31.213
S7: scheduled runs watch it
with Defender for Identity;

203
00:13:31.213 --> 00:13:33.873
a G M S A nobody asked for is drift.

204
00:13:33.873 --> 00:13:36.411
S8: the two owners certify it.

205
00:13:36.411 --> 00:13:39.433
S9: an owner's departure
starts a transfer;

206
00:13:39.433 --> 00:13:43.522
an orphan is disabled, then
has thirty days to be claimed.

207
00:13:43.522 --> 00:13:49.205
S10: after the parallel run the pipeline
disables the old account and moves it to

208
00:13:49.205 --> 00:13:52.563
the holding O U; after
ninety days with no sign-in,

209
00:13:52.563 --> 00:13:55.060
a person uninstalls and deletes it.

210
00:13:55.060 --> 00:13:58.685
S11: when the workload moves, Migration C.

211
00:13:58.685 --> 00:14:02.978
Two things aren't verified yet:
ServiceNow's Active Directory spoke,

212
00:14:02.978 --> 00:14:08.230
so ServiceNow records and the plan writes,
and SailPoint's export field names.

213
00:14:11.980 --> 00:14:18.556
Migration A moves a user account to a
G M S A, in Microsoft Learn's order:

214
00:14:18.556 --> 00:14:23.605
K D S root key, create, install,
switch, validate, retire.

215
00:14:23.605 --> 00:14:28.976
A1: read the account with Get-A D User,
its S P Ns, delegation and groups,

216
00:14:28.976 --> 00:14:34.543
and on each host read the service's
logon account with S C query config.

217
00:14:34.543 --> 00:14:38.318
A2: the forest needs one K D S root key.

218
00:14:38.318 --> 00:14:42.925
If there's none, a Domain Admin or
Enterprise Admin creates it once,

219
00:14:42.925 --> 00:14:47.021
and Learn's K D S page says
domain controllers wait up to ten

220
00:14:47.021 --> 00:14:51.117
hours before they allow a G M S
A, so the key replicates first.

221
00:14:51.117 --> 00:14:54.682
Learn offers a key backdated
ten hours for a test domain

222
00:14:54.682 --> 00:14:58.168
with one domain controller;
never use it in production.

223
00:14:58.168 --> 00:15:01.498
And Learn's containers
page says more than one

224
00:15:01.498 --> 00:15:04.917
key makes G M S As fail
after a password rotation.

225
00:15:04.917 --> 00:15:10.288
A3: create a group of the hosts, then
the G M S A with A E S two-fifty-six,

226
00:15:10.288 --> 00:15:14.342
which Learn's overview
says to always configure.

227
00:15:14.342 --> 00:15:19.260
Its name must be unique in the forest,
and its password interval can be set

228
00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:23.931
only now; the standard sets the name,
the O U and the one retriever group.

229
00:15:23.931 --> 00:15:26.246
For a workload that holds no S P N,

230
00:15:26.246 --> 00:15:31.680
the program's default is Learn's
outbound-only variant; record that choice.

231
00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:37.233
And the program creates G M S As under a
delegated create right, which Learn allows,

232
00:15:37.233 --> 00:15:40.465
not as a Domain Admin: the
creator group holds it,

233
00:15:40.465 --> 00:15:42.868
and the pipeline is its only member.

234
00:15:42.868 --> 00:15:47.331
A4: after a restart, install
it on each host and test it.

235
00:15:47.331 --> 00:15:52.430
Test-A D Service Account must return
true on every host before you switch.

236
00:15:56.180 --> 00:16:01.116
A5 happens in one change window,
because an S P N left on the old account

237
00:16:01.116 --> 00:16:05.793
while the service runs as the G M
S A breaks Kerberos to the service.

238
00:16:05.793 --> 00:16:08.779
Each S P N must have exactly one owner.

239
00:16:08.779 --> 00:16:14.930
Remove it from the old account, add it to
the G M S A, and list it with set S P N.

240
00:16:14.930 --> 00:16:16.918
Then change the workload's identity.

241
00:16:16.918 --> 00:16:19.776
A G M S A logs on with a blank password,

242
00:16:19.776 --> 00:16:23.718
which Windows fills in, as
Learn's Entra page says.

243
00:16:23.718 --> 00:16:26.894
For a Windows service, S
C config sets the account,

244
00:16:26.894 --> 00:16:29.992
and the managed-account
query must report true.

245
00:16:29.992 --> 00:16:34.108
A scheduled task gets a new principal
with the password logon type,

246
00:16:34.108 --> 00:16:37.550
as Learn's assessment pages do
it, and an I I S application

247
00:16:37.550 --> 00:16:40.917
pool gets identity type three
with the blank password.

248
00:16:40.917 --> 00:16:43.870
The guide notes that
Learn has no single page

249
00:16:43.870 --> 00:16:46.743
showing that last one
end to end, so verify it.

250
00:16:46.743 --> 00:16:50.218
A6 is the thirty-day parallel
run: test end to end,

251
00:16:50.218 --> 00:16:54.229
keep the old account enabled,
and watch its logons stop.

252
00:16:54.229 --> 00:16:59.374
A7 disables the old account and moves
it to the holding organizational unit;

253
00:16:59.374 --> 00:17:03.130
Chapter 6 plans both as one
step, and the pipeline runs it.

254
00:17:03.130 --> 00:17:07.412
Deletion comes only after ninety
days disabled with no sign-in seen,

255
00:17:07.412 --> 00:17:09.630
through the decommission process.

256
00:17:09.630 --> 00:17:12.945
To roll back, switch the
workload back with the old

257
00:17:12.945 --> 00:17:16.370
password from the vault,
and move the S P Ns back.

258
00:17:20.120 --> 00:17:23.298
Migration B moves the
same kind of account to a

259
00:17:23.298 --> 00:17:27.395
delegated managed service
account, on Windows Server 2025.

260
00:17:27.395 --> 00:17:32.432
A D M S A supersedes the old account:
once the migration completes,

261
00:17:32.432 --> 00:17:37.944
the old password can't be used, its
S P Ns, delegation and authentication

262
00:17:37.944 --> 00:17:42.220
policy move to the D M S A, and
the old account is disabled.

263
00:17:42.220 --> 00:17:44.670
Check Microsoft Learn's limits first.

264
00:17:44.670 --> 00:17:48.279
At least one Windows Server
2025 domain controller,

265
00:17:48.279 --> 00:17:52.811
and every machine that uses the
account updated to support D M S As;

266
00:17:52.811 --> 00:17:56.420
one that isn't fails once
the old account is disabled.

267
00:17:56.420 --> 00:18:00.231
You can't migrate an M S A
or a G M S A to a D M S A,

268
00:18:00.231 --> 00:18:04.620
and unconstrained
delegation stops working.

269
00:18:04.620 --> 00:18:07.576
Learn's minimum to create
one is Domain Admins,

270
00:18:07.576 --> 00:18:10.220
Enterprise Admins or equivalent rights.

271
00:18:10.220 --> 00:18:12.975
After the attack called BadSuccessor,

272
00:18:12.975 --> 00:18:17.883
where anyone who could create a D M
S A could take over another account,

273
00:18:17.883 --> 00:18:20.982
the program is stricter:
Domain Admins only,

274
00:18:20.982 --> 00:18:26.320
and the August 2025 update on every
Windows Server 2025 domain controller.

275
00:18:26.320 --> 00:18:27.661
B1 creates the D M S A.

276
00:18:27.661 --> 00:18:33.275
Learn's set-up page then sets a state
attribute with a parameter the cmdlet doesn't

277
00:18:33.275 --> 00:18:38.470
have; for a migration, skip it, because
starting the migration sets the state.

278
00:18:38.470 --> 00:18:41.511
B2 starts the migration,
and on each host sets

279
00:18:41.511 --> 00:18:45.045
Learn's registry value and
restarts the one service.

280
00:18:45.045 --> 00:18:48.846
B3: Learn says to wait at
least two ticket lifetimes,

281
00:18:48.846 --> 00:18:51.470
fourteen days, and recommends four.

282
00:18:51.470 --> 00:18:54.095
The program's thirty days covers four.

283
00:18:54.095 --> 00:18:57.590
Then complete it, and never
delete the original account.

284
00:19:01.340 --> 00:19:04.869
Migration C starts when the
workload moves to Azure,

285
00:19:04.869 --> 00:19:10.040
or gains a call to Graph or an Azure A P I,
and only for what it reaches with OAuth.

286
00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:15.178
Microsoft Learn's security best practices
for application properties set the order:

287
00:19:15.178 --> 00:19:19.449
a managed identity whenever possible;
where the app doesn't run on Azure,

288
00:19:19.449 --> 00:19:23.357
a platform that manages its own
credentials, which is federation;

289
00:19:23.357 --> 00:19:27.192
otherwise a certificate from a
trusted certificate authority,

290
00:19:27.192 --> 00:19:30.015
kept in a vault; and never
a password credential.

291
00:19:30.015 --> 00:19:34.562
C1: a workload moving to Azure gets
a user-assigned managed identity,

292
00:19:34.562 --> 00:19:39.840
created with the Azure C L I and given
the narrowest role at the narrowest scope.

293
00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:43.003
It outlives its resources,
so it needs an owner.

294
00:19:43.003 --> 00:19:47.564
C2: on an Arc-enabled server,
use the server's own identity.

295
00:19:47.564 --> 00:19:51.851
Learn says only local
administrators and one local group

296
00:19:51.851 --> 00:19:55.602
can request its tokens, so
add the G M S A to that group.

297
00:19:55.602 --> 00:19:57.805
C3: on a host with no Arc agent,

298
00:19:57.805 --> 00:20:02.690
an app registration with a
certificate, with two named owners.

299
00:20:02.690 --> 00:20:06.966
C4: for C I C D or another
cloud, a federated credential,

300
00:20:06.966 --> 00:20:11.440
whose issuer, subject and
audience must match exactly.

301
00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:16.752
C5: after thirty days carrying traffic,
retire what the cloud path replaced.

302
00:20:16.752 --> 00:20:21.968
Disable it, or, for a G M S A the workload
no longer needs, record its retirement,

303
00:20:21.968 --> 00:20:24.829
and a person uninstalls it from its hosts

304
00:20:24.829 --> 00:20:27.941
before the decommission
process deletes it.

305
00:20:27.941 --> 00:20:31.010
The code never uninstalls or deletes.

306
00:20:34.760 --> 00:20:37.032
One more move the guide covers,

307
00:20:37.032 --> 00:20:40.848
because mail breaks when an
account becomes a G M S A.

308
00:20:40.848 --> 00:20:46.986
A G M S A has no mailbox and no
password an S M T P client could type.

309
00:20:46.986 --> 00:20:52.368
And Microsoft Learn says basic-authentication
S M T P submission to Exchange Online

310
00:20:52.368 --> 00:20:57.598
is scheduled for deprecation, so a stored
mailbox password isn't the answer either.

311
00:20:57.598 --> 00:20:59.472
Choose by where the mail server is.

312
00:20:59.472 --> 00:21:05.312
On-premises Exchange: a dedicated receive
connector that offers integrated Windows

313
00:21:05.312 --> 00:21:10.247
authentication, Kerberos, and Send As
on a shared mailbox, never a person's.

314
00:21:10.247 --> 00:21:11.248
That's M1.

315
00:21:11.248 --> 00:21:14.614
A client that can't authenticate
gets an anonymous relay

316
00:21:14.614 --> 00:21:18.123
limited to the workload's hosts'
addresses, the weakest path.

317
00:21:18.123 --> 00:21:19.161
That's M2.

318
00:21:19.161 --> 00:21:25.045
Exchange Online: the workload sends through
Microsoft Graph send mail as a workload

319
00:21:25.045 --> 00:21:29.922
identity, the app with a certificate
from C3, or the Arc identity from C2,

320
00:21:29.922 --> 00:21:33.873
scoped to one shared mailbox
with R B A C for Applications.

321
00:21:33.873 --> 00:21:36.439
Don't consent Mail dot Send in Entra I D:

322
00:21:36.439 --> 00:21:41.323
Learn says the two grants are a union,
so an Entra grant removes the scope.

323
00:21:41.323 --> 00:21:43.297
Test the scope both ways.

324
00:21:43.297 --> 00:21:46.758
In GCC, send mail runs on
the worldwide Graph host,

325
00:21:46.758 --> 00:21:52.122
but Learn doesn't yet specify R B A C
for Applications for government clouds,

326
00:21:52.122 --> 00:21:55.236
so test it in the tenant
before relying on it.

327
00:21:55.236 --> 00:21:58.820
High Volume Email is
listed for Worldwide only.

328
00:22:02.570 --> 00:22:05.657
The guide lists the dangers
and the guard for each.

329
00:22:05.657 --> 00:22:11.049
Create a G M S A before the K D S root
key replicates, and password retrieval

330
00:22:11.049 --> 00:22:16.170
fails on hosts that ask another domain
controller: wait the ten hours.

331
00:22:16.170 --> 00:22:19.642
Create a second root key,
and G M S As fail after

332
00:22:19.642 --> 00:22:23.395
their next password
rotation: one key per forest.

333
00:22:23.395 --> 00:22:27.969
Leave an S P N on the old account,
and Kerberos to the service

334
00:22:27.969 --> 00:22:32.733
fails or falls back to N T L M: move
the S P Ns in the switch window.

335
00:22:32.733 --> 00:22:35.759
Switch a host that can't
retrieve the password,

336
00:22:35.759 --> 00:22:38.945
and the service won't start:
test every host first.

337
00:22:38.945 --> 00:22:41.220
Put a G M S A in a privileged group,

338
00:22:41.220 --> 00:22:45.945
and every host that retrieves its
password holds that privilege.

339
00:22:45.945 --> 00:22:51.510
Complete a D M S A migration too early, or
with a host not on Windows Server 2025,

340
00:22:51.510 --> 00:22:55.220
and the service fails on
the hosts it hasn't learned.

341
00:22:55.220 --> 00:22:59.070
Delete the old account during the
migration, and there's no way back.

342
00:22:59.070 --> 00:23:01.901
Put a client secret on
the new app registration,

343
00:23:01.901 --> 00:23:05.095
and you have a password in
a configuration file again.

344
00:23:05.095 --> 00:23:08.511
Run a G M S A container on a
host that isn't domain-joined,

345
00:23:08.511 --> 00:23:13.324
and Learn's pattern for it keeps a stored
domain password again; the program

346
00:23:13.324 --> 00:23:18.370
allows domain-joined hosts only, and
anything else needs a reviewed exception.

347
00:23:18.370 --> 00:23:22.133
And a G M S A left behind
when Active Directory retires

348
00:23:22.133 --> 00:23:26.340
loses its credential with
the last domain controller.

349
00:23:30.090 --> 00:23:33.485
In production, the same
moves run as reviewed code,

350
00:23:33.485 --> 00:23:36.715
in the Non-Person Identity
developer workbook.

351
00:23:36.715 --> 00:23:39.169
Chapter 12 runs the standard's checks,

352
00:23:39.169 --> 00:23:42.178
from the request to the
ninety-day delete list.

353
00:23:42.178 --> 00:23:45.177
Chapter 3 records each
account's disposition.

354
00:23:45.177 --> 00:23:48.727
Chapter 6 does Migration A as
a plan: create the G M S A,

355
00:23:48.727 --> 00:23:53.798
wait for the host change and the thirty
days, then disable the superseded

356
00:23:53.798 --> 00:23:59.377
account and move it to the holding
organizational unit, and the pipeline runs it.

357
00:23:59.377 --> 00:24:04.241
It does Migration B as three planned
actions, create, start and complete,

358
00:24:04.241 --> 00:24:09.764
with every limit Microsoft Learn sets
checked first, and a Domain Admin runs them.

359
00:24:09.764 --> 00:24:13.941
Chapter 5 does Migration C, the
identity and its credential,

360
00:24:13.941 --> 00:24:18.628
and tests a real token exchange,
and Chapter 7 cuts its permissions.

361
00:24:18.628 --> 00:24:21.458
Chapter 6 also keeps
the island list and the

362
00:24:21.458 --> 00:24:24.289
reconciliation for what stays on Kerberos.

363
00:24:24.289 --> 00:24:26.865
What the code never does matters as much.

364
00:24:26.865 --> 00:24:32.227
It never runs a plan nobody approved,
because each plan is sent only by its hash.

365
00:24:32.227 --> 00:24:36.949
And it never creates a K D S root
key, never runs undo or reset,

366
00:24:36.949 --> 00:24:40.490
and never uninstalls
or deletes an account.

367
00:24:40.490 --> 00:24:45.510
To try it, take Modules 12 and 13 of
the Non-Person Identity learning path:

368
00:24:45.510 --> 00:24:50.190
the migration, and the standard, on
the kit's fixtures, with no tenant.

369
00:24:50.190 --> 00:24:55.590
Then Chapter 6's second lab, in a test
domain, and the guide's checklist.

370
00:24:55.590 --> 00:24:56.420
Thank you.
